WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.124
Most cues in a prompt book are complete: LX 14 GO.

2
00:00:06.124 --> 00:00:08.362
A few say only: ask the director.

3
00:00:08.362 --> 00:00:12.770
On the first night, the stage manager reaches that cue, and asks.

4
00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:16.703
The director decides, and the answer goes into the margin.

5
00:00:16.703 --> 00:00:21.518
From then on, the stage manager reads the margin, and never asks again.

6
00:00:21.518 --> 00:00:23.552
Which suits the director fine.

7
00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:27.318
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:27.318 --> 00:00:30.294
Every call in my code names the method it calls.

9
00:00:30.294 --> 00:00:32.649
So how can a call not know its target?

10
00:00:32.649 --> 00:00:36.803
And if it has to work it out, doesn't it do that every single time?

11
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:40.322
Let's start from a call you know.

12
00:00:40.322 --> 00:00:44.519
In the last lesson, main called seconds with invokestatic.

13
00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:50.743
The instruction names its target exactly: class StackDemo, method seconds, type J I J.

14
00:00:50.743 --> 00:00:52.915
A complete cue, like LX 14 GO.

15
00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.554
Now a line we write all the time.

16
00:00:55.554 --> 00:01:04.600
A method reference, IndyTour::add, stored as an IntBinaryOperator: a function that takes two ints and gives back an int.

17
00:01:04.733 --> 00:01:08.293
javap shows what javac wrote for it: invokedynamic.

18
00:01:08.293 --> 00:01:10.177
And look at what's missing.

19
00:01:10.177 --> 00:01:13.318
It has a method name, applyAsInt, and a type.

20
00:01:13.318 --> 00:01:14.504
No class to call.

21
00:01:14.504 --> 00:01:18.761
Just a number, pointing into a table called BootstrapMethods.

22
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:23.275
Read the type from the point of view of the code around it.

23
00:01:23.275 --> 00:01:27.618
Take nothing off the operand stack, and put an IntBinaryOperator on it.

24
00:01:27.618 --> 00:01:29.453
Remember the stagehand's pile?

25
00:01:29.453 --> 00:01:31.838
This cue says: put one new card on top.

26
00:01:31.838 --> 00:01:35.508
How to make that card is written nowhere in your class file.

27
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.347
That's the cue that says: ask the director.

28
00:01:39.347 --> 00:01:45.311
The director is the bootstrap method, here LambdaMetafactory.metafactory, from the JDK.

29
00:01:45.311 --> 00:01:54.084
Below it are its static arguments: the interface method's type, the method that does the work, IndyTour.add, and the type again.

30
00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:56.568
So what happens on the first run?

31
00:01:56.568 --> 00:02:00.263
The JVM calls the bootstrap method, with four things.

32
00:02:00.263 --> 00:02:03.820
A Lookup, which carries your class's access rights.

33
00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:04.447
The name.

34
00:02:04.447 --> 00:02:05.075
The type.

35
00:02:05.075 --> 00:02:06.957
And those static arguments.

36
00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:12.028
The bootstrap method returns a CallSite: an object that holds a target.

37
00:02:12.028 --> 00:02:20.438
The target is a method handle, a typed reference to a method that the JVM can call directly, much like a function pointer.

38
00:02:20.438 --> 00:02:23.747
The JVM links the instruction to that call site.

39
00:02:23.747 --> 00:02:25.677
The answer is in the margin.

40
00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:28.752
Every run after that calls the target directly.

41
00:02:28.752 --> 00:02:32.375
The bootstrap method never runs again for that instruction.

42
00:02:32.375 --> 00:02:34.953
The director gets the rest of the run off.

43
00:02:35.867 --> 00:02:40.777
If invokedynamic had an honest name, it would be called invoke, ask once.

44
00:02:40.777 --> 00:02:43.603
Dynamic sounds like it changes every time.

45
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.966
So the point is: it's decided once, at first use.

46
00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:49.808
After that, it's as plain as any other call.

47
00:02:50.733 --> 00:02:53.099
Let's see the answer being reused.

48
00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:56.369
greeter returns a lambda that captures nothing.

49
00:02:56.369 --> 00:02:57.691
Call greeter twice.

50
00:02:57.691 --> 00:02:59.083
Same object, or two?

51
00:02:59.083 --> 00:03:00.196
Pause and guess.

52
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:08.303
The lesson's IndyTour printed: captures nothing, same object both times?

53
00:03:08.303 --> 00:03:08.657
true.

54
00:03:08.657 --> 00:03:13.972
The call site was linked once, and its target hands back one shared object.

55
00:03:14.867 --> 00:03:16.710
I know what you're thinking.

56
00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.870
A call that doesn't know its target sounds slow.

57
00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:25.136
But LambdaMetafactory returns a ConstantCallSite, whose target can never change.

58
00:03:25.136 --> 00:03:26.124
Written in ink.

59
00:03:26.124 --> 00:03:34.484
So the JIT, the compiler inside the JVM, can compile straight through it, as if the instruction had named its target all along.

60
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.283
And here the picture needs one correction.

61
00:03:38.283 --> 00:03:43.087
A real margin note is in pencil, and a director can change their mind.

62
00:03:43.087 --> 00:03:47.068
For languages that need that, the JDK has MutableCallSite.

63
00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:54.027
The lesson's CallSiteDemo links a call site to inSeconds, and calls it with 120: 120 seconds.

64
00:03:54.027 --> 00:03:59.770
It changes the target to inMinutes, and calls the very same invoker: 2 minutes.

65
00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:05.949
Then it tries the same on a ConstantCallSite, and gets UnsupportedOperationException.

66
00:04:05.949 --> 00:04:07.112
Linked for good.

67
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.970
Why go to all this trouble?

68
00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:14.639
invokedynamic arrived in Java 7, for other languages on the JVM.

69
00:04:14.639 --> 00:04:17.704
javac first used it for lambdas in Java 8.

70
00:04:17.704 --> 00:04:23.030
It moves a decision out of your class files, and into the JDK you run on.

71
00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:29.815
A newer JDK can do it better, and your old class files pick that up without being recompiled.

72
00:04:30.733 --> 00:04:35.516
Kotlin compiles lambdas the same way, by default since Kotlin 2.0.

73
00:04:35.516 --> 00:04:37.907
Here's greeter in both languages.

74
00:04:37.907 --> 00:04:40.588
Each lambda becomes an invokedynamic.

75
00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:42.397
Now the names.

76
00:04:42.397 --> 00:04:46.847
The cue that says ask the director is an invokedynamic instruction.

77
00:04:46.847 --> 00:04:49.305
The director is its bootstrap method.

78
00:04:49.305 --> 00:04:51.497
The margin note is the call site.

79
00:04:51.497 --> 00:04:55.217
And what's written on it is the target, a method handle.

80
00:04:56.133 --> 00:04:56.962
A few rules.

81
00:04:56.962 --> 00:05:02.277
One: the director is asked once per call site, that is, once per instruction.

82
00:05:02.277 --> 00:05:06.073
Two lambdas on two lines are two cues, asked once each.

83
00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:10.556
Two: don't avoid lambdas because you fear a lookup on every call.

84
00:05:10.556 --> 00:05:11.612
There isn't one.

85
00:05:11.612 --> 00:05:16.958
Three: javap shows the bootstrap method and its arguments, but not what it built.

86
00:05:16.958 --> 00:05:19.136
To see that, you run the program.

87
00:05:20.067 --> 00:05:22.718
So, how can a call not know its target?

88
00:05:22.718 --> 00:05:25.642
It carries a question instead of an answer.

89
00:05:25.642 --> 00:05:30.673
The first time it runs, the JDK answers, and the answer is written in ink.

90
00:05:30.673 --> 00:05:33.800
An invokedynamic is linked once, at first use.

91
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.908
After that, it's a direct call.

92
00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:36.878
Quick check.

93
00:05:36.878 --> 00:05:40.191
A loop runs the same lambda line a million times.

94
00:05:40.191 --> 00:05:43.233
How many times does its bootstrap method run?

95
00:05:46.267 --> 00:05:46.627
Once.

96
00:05:46.627 --> 00:05:49.080
The first run links the call site.

97
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.111
Every other run calls the target directly.
